  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1156 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nGoing into the weekend, the next system to monitor will be a \nsurface low that tracks through the southern Mississippi River \nValley on into the Ohio River Valley Saturday into Sunday. This \nmid to upper level system is still out over the eastern Pacific,\nbut the long term models all have a fairly consistent beat on \nhow things will play out with the precipitation timing Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. The better chances for the precip \nwill be to the south along the WI/IL border, closer to the \ndeeper forcing as a couple of systems phase together. \n\nThe challenge with this system will be the precipitation type \nas it will mainly depend on how warm our surface temps are. \nGFS/RRFS soundings from Janesville show a warm layer near the \nsurface with temps in the low 40s while the ECMWF shows temps \naround 37F. The 850mb low tracks well to the south of the region\nwith those low level temps being below 0C throughout the precip\nphase of the system. So, signals are starting to trend toward\nthis being more of a potential snow scenario, albeit with above\nfreezing surface temps. That said, we're looking at about 0.10\"\nof QPF with lower end snow to liquid ratios (6:1?) with warm\nground...occurring on a late Saturday night into Sunday morning\nsituation. So, not an overly concerning situation but\npotentially our first accumulating snow...moreso if we trend\nto the colder/Euro side.\n\nBeyond that, colder air pushes down briefly into Monday behind\nthe system but then the low level flow goes back to southerly\nafter a broad ridge of high pressure tracks eastward across the\nmid-Atlantic. Low chances for some WAA precip Tuesday with\nanother, deeper trough coming in later toward the week to watch\nfor more precip.\n\nHalbach",
